---
name: Appwrite Edge
description: The router and orchestrator that run customer code for Appwrite Sites and Functions in six regions.
github: https://github.com/open-runtimes/orchestrator
---

Appwrite Edge runs customer code in six regions. The router decides where each request goes, and the orchestrator runs the code.

## The router

Every Sites domain needed something in front of it that knew which deployment to serve. I built that router for the Sites launch in 2025, and it now handles about 11 million requests a day.

A request comes in for a domain. The router looks up the deployment and its region, then finds a runtime to send it to. Preview deployments, redirect rules, custom error pages and cache invalidation all live here. When a customer ships a new deployment, the region publishes an event and the edge drops its cached rule and runtime. Each project's runtimes scale on their own, and spare nodes hold warm capacity so a spike doesn't wait for a new machine.

## open-runtimes/orchestrator

The old executor drove Docker on a single host. open-runtimes/orchestrator is the next generation orchestrator for Appwrite Functions and builds, and it already runs Appwrite Cloud's builds in production. I wrote the design docs and nearly all of the code in 2026.

One API runs four kinds of workload. Deployments serve traffic. Pools keep warm pods ready. Sandboxes run untrusted code alone. Jobs run builds. The same API runs on Docker Compose locally and on Kubernetes in production.

Build artifacts get the most interesting treatment. The orchestrator mounts them as filesystem images instead of extracting them, so a large deployment starts without unpacking thousands of files. A node mount agent does the mount on the host, which means no pod running customer code needs a privileged container sitting next to it.
